  "revision": 0,
  "pipelines": [
    {
      "type": "implementation",
      "status": "PASS",
      "started_at": "2026-05-20T09:52:44Z",
      "completed_at": "2026-05-20T09:54:05Z",
      "duration_ms": 81000,
      "summary": [
        "Added one line after the setInterval(...) call inside the if (runStatusFilename) block in orchestrator.js: `_orchLogPreviewCleanups.push(function () { clearInterval(statusPollTimer); });`. This registers the statusPollTimer interval for cleanup when renderOrchestrator() re-renders, preventing orphaned intervals. All 2204 tests pass with no regressions."
      ],
      "artifacts": {
        "files_modified": [
          "mcp-server/gui/public/views/orchestrator.js"
        ]
      },
      "metrics": {
        "tests_passed": 2204,
        "tests_failed": 0
      },
      "comments": [
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-05-20T09:54:05Z",
          "note": "The statusPollTimer interval already self-cancels inside the callback (via clearInterval(statusPollTimer) when status resolves or MAX_STATUS_POLLS is reached). The new _orchLogPreviewCleanups entry provides a safety net for the case where the user navigates away or re-renders before the timer self-terminates. This dual-cancel pattern is correct and intentional."
        },
        {
          "type": "debt",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-05-20T09:54:05Z",
          "note": "orchestrator.js lines 180–181: refreshQueue() also drains _orchLogPreviewCleanups directly (not just renderOrchestrator()). This means the statusPollTimer cleanup registered after startBtn click will also be cleared by the next queue refresh — which may clear it prematurely if a queue poll fires before the status poll resolves. This is pre-existing behaviour and out of scope for this WP, but worth noting for future review."
        }
      ]
    },
